<section xml:id="section-triple-integrals-rectangular" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Triple Integrals in Rectangular Coordinates</title>

<p>
Previously, we learned how to calculate double integrals and how to use
them to calculate volumes. Today, we will learn triple integrals in
Cartesian coordinates, which can also be used to evaluate volumes of
solids, along with other applications that we will learn.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-triple-integral-meaning">
<title>What Does a Triple Integral Mean?</title>

<p>
An important application comes first: the mass of a block <m>B</m>
with variable density (mass per unit volume) <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-mass-triple">
M = \iiint_B \rho(x,y,z)\,dV.
</men>

<p>
More generally, a <term>triple integral</term> has the form
<m>\iiint_E f(x,y,z)\,dV</m>, defined by chopping the solid <m>E</m>
into tiny blocks of volume
<m>\Delta V = \Delta x\,\Delta y\,\Delta z</m>, sampling <m>f</m> in
each block, and passing to the limit. A natural question is: what does
<m>\iiint_E dV</m> mean? We can think of it as summing the volumes of
the tiny blocks <m>\Delta V</m>, hence
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-volume-triple">
\text{Volume of } E = \iiint_E dV.
</men>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-triple-case1">
<title>Case I: <m>E = \{(x,y,z) : a \le x \le b,</m>
<m>g_1(x) \le y \le g_2(x),</m>
<m>u_1(x,y) \le z \le u_2(x,y)\}</m></title>

<p>
The first case that we will study is the case where the region of
integration is of the form
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
E = \{(x,y,z) : \amp a \le x \le b,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp g_1(x) \le y \le g_2(x),
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp u_1(x,y) \le z \le u_2(x,y)\},
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
so <m>E</m> lies between the surfaces <m>z = u_1(x,y)</m> and
<m>z = u_2(x,y)</m> over a projection <m>D</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, and
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \iiint_E f(x,y,z)\,dV
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \int_a^b\!\!\int_{g_1(x)}^{g_2(x)}\!\!\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-triple-iterated" number="yes">
\amp \qquad f(x,y,z)\,dz\,dy\,dx.
</mrow>
</md>

<example xml:id="example-triple-tetrahedron">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the triple integral
<m>\displaystyle\iiint_E z\,dx\,dy\,dz</m>, where <m>E</m> is the
tetrahedron shown in <xref ref="fig-triple-tetra-tikz"/>, bounded by
the coordinate planes and the plane <m>z = 1 - x - y</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Example I.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-triple-tetra-tikz">
<caption>
The tetrahedron <m>E</m> of <xref ref="example-triple-tetrahedron"/>,
bounded by the three coordinate planes and the plane
<m>z = 1 - x - y</m>. Its projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane is the
triangle <m>D</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A tetrahedron cut from the first octant by a slanted plane, with its shadow triangle shaded.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a tetrahedron whose vertices are
the origin and the three points one zero zero, zero one zero, and zero
zero one. The slanted face is the plane z equals one minus x minus y.
The base triangle D in the x y plane is shaded.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
