<subsection xml:id="sec-triple-mass">
<title>Mass With Variable Density</title>

<example xml:id="example-triple-mass">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Assume that the density (mass per unit volume) is given by
<m>\rho(x,y,z) = x</m>. Evaluate the mass <m>M</m> of the solid
<m>R</m> of <xref ref="fig-triple-wedge-tikz"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Example III.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-triple-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The first five questions all concern the same solid <m>E</m>, the region
bounded below by the paraboloid <m>z = x^2+y^2</m> and above by the upper
half <m>z = 1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> of the sphere of radius <m>1</m>
centered at <m>(0,0,1)</m>, drawn in
<xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>. Four of them ask for a sign or
a yes-or-no answer rather than a number: the point is to read the
integrand on the region, not to integrate.
</p>

<p>
It is worth locating <m>E</m> first. Find where the two surfaces meet, and describe <m>E</m>: over which region of the
<m>xy</m>-plane does it sit, and between which surfaces does <m>z</m>
run?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,6.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for locating the solid E.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-sign-one">
<title>The sign of an integral, without calculating</title>
<statement>
<p>
<term>Without calculation</term>, decide whether the integral below is
positive, negative, or zero. The region of integration <m>E</m> is the
region bounded by <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> and
<m>z=1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>.
</p>
<me>
\iiint_E \left(z-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}-1\right) dV
</me>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>Positive</p></li>
<li><p>Negative</p></li>
<li><p>Zero</p></li>
</ol>
<p>
<em>Hint</em>: begin by determining the limits of integration.
</p>

