<exercise xml:id="q-triple-two-cylinders">
<title>A volume between two cylinders</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a triple integral for computing the volume of the region bounded by
the two cylinders <m>y=x^2</m> and <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and the two planes
<m>z=0</m> and <m>z=\frac13(x+y+1)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-triple-two-cylinders"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the volume between the two cylinders.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-triple-two-cylinders">
<caption>
Figure (4): the solid bounded by the cylinders <m>y=x^2</m> and
<m>y=\sqrt{x}</m>, the plane <m>z=0</m>, and the plane
<m>z=\frac13(x+y+1)</m>, whose two edges are drawn on the walls.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="af2ud86q"
             width="100%" aspect="600:600" design-width="600"
             material-width="600" material-height="600"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. Looking
straight down shows the region <m>D</m> between the two curves in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, which is the shadow the triple integral is built over.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

